# Dev DB vs EF Baseline — Structural Diff (Pre-Stamp Gate)

Date: 2026-06-04
Branch: feature/code-first-migrations
Question: Does the **Dev** database schema match what the EF Core baseline + migrations
produce, so Dev can be "stamped" (adopt code-first) as-is — or must it be reconciled first?

Method (read-only):
  1. Built the EF schema FROM SCRATCH on a throwaway PG16 container (`slx-devcheck`, port 55436):
       - `dotnet ef database update` → migrations `20260603164745_Baseline` + `20260603172408_AddAuditChangeLog` (BOTH applied cleanly, no errors)
       - applied `database/procedures/*` (40 files, manifest order) + `database/repeatable/views/*` (23 files)
       - `pg_dump --schema-only --no-owner --no-privileges -n mes -n sc -n common -n application -n pm`
         → `database/audit/ef-built.schema.sql`  (107 base tables in the 5 schemas; audit schema EXCLUDED by the -n allow-list, same as the Dev dump)
  2. Loaded `dev.schema.sql` (cmp_dev), `ef-built.schema.sql` (cmp_ef), and `uat.schema.sql` (cmp_uat, ground truth) into separate DBs on the same container.
  3. Compared by SETS of structural facts via information_schema / pg_catalog (name-independent),
     NOT raw text diff. Restricted to the 107 baseline-managed tables; EXCLUDED the 3 unmanaged
     tables Dev carries (`application.employee_department`, `sc.item`, `common.fcm_token`) and the
     `audit` schema (AddAuditChangeLog creates it idempotently; Dev legitimately lacks it).

Legend per delta: EXPECTED (deliberate baseline choice / exclusion-list / cosmetic) | BLOCKING (real
mismatch that makes the EF model disagree with Dev's reality once stamped).

================================================================================
0. HEADLINE — premise reversal
================================================================================
The task brief assumed "the baseline was proven to MATCH UAT" and that Dev might lag the baseline.
The live rebuild shows the OPPOSITE: **Dev faithfully matches UAT; it is the current EF baseline
(`20260603164745_Baseline`) that diverges from UAT** on a large, systematic surface:

  - 92 columns differ between EF-built and Dev — and Dev == UAT on ALL 92.
  - 19 FK edges differ in ON DELETE action / NOT VALID flag — and Dev == UAT on all sampled.
  - 1 CHECK constraint differs — here Dev is the outlier vs UAT (see §4).

The prior `baseline-vs-uat.diff` "MATCH" verdict (which only spot-checked mes.part + common.document
nullability) did NOT surface this full surface. The pattern is classic EF-convention drift: the C#
model maps DateTime→timestamptz, non-nullable CLR props→NOT NULL, int→integer, long→bigint, and
omits DB-side sequence/literal DEFAULTs — none of which round-trips the actual UAT/Dev shape.

